const tips = [
	{
		copy:
			"Prepare your trash early. Brook ST01-011 is one of the best cards to place there, especially by using it as a counter first. During your combo, Pirates Docking Six can bring it back so you can attach a DON!! to the leader and make Sabo's effect easier to turn on.",
		cards: [brook, piratesDockingSix, saboLeader],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Use Pirates Docking Six to pick the right target for the moment. It can bring back Nami for another search, Zoro when you need a blocker, or Brook when you want the extra DON!! on the leader. The target shouldn't be automatic; it should match the board state.",
		cards: [piratesDockingSix, nami, zoroBlocker, brook],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Sabo's effect is the engine that turns your board from decent to oppressive. Once it's online, the +1000 power also applies during the opposing turn. That makes Pirates Docking Six harder to remove and turns even your small characters into meaningful attackers and defenders. Since Pirates Docking Six is the only card in the list that enables the leader effect, protecting it matters a lot.",
		cards: [saboLeader, piratesDockingSix],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Take advantage of the wide board once it's assembled. The deck gets especially dangerous into 5000-power leaders because your boosted smaller characters suddenly become real attackers. On the other hand, leaders that naturally sit at 6000 power or higher make that plan less efficient.",
		cards: [nami, brook, sanjiSt31],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"ST21-003 Sanji is mainly an offensive Pirates Docking Six target. In real games, its main job is usually giving Unblockable to Pirates Docking Six so your pressure converts immediately.",
		cards: [sanjiRush, piratesDockingSix],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Don't neglect your Life total just because your board is harder to break once Sabo is active. The deck still needs enough room to keep building and enough resources left to defend the turns that matter.",
		cards: [saboLeader],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"In the late game, shift from pure development into board control. Your cost-7 Luffys and Gum-Gum Mole Pistol become your main tools for KO'ing opposing characters or dropping them low enough to attack over. If you can grant Rush to Luffy, it can sometimes handle a second target in the same turn.",
		cards: [luffySt31, molePistol],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Time for the Counterattack depends directly on Pirates Docking Six. The event can give you 4000 counter for 1 DON!!, but it becomes dead if Docking Six isn't in play. The good news is that once Docking Six sticks, it's usually hard enough to remove that the event stays live.",
		cards: [counterattack, piratesDockingSix],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Ground Death has two separate jobs. In hand, it can blank the effect of an opposing cost-5-or-lower character, especially a blocker or an On K.O. threat. Once you have ten or more cards in trash, it can also give +4000 counter to your leader or one of your characters.",
		cards: [groundDeath],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Bad Manners Kick Course turns awkward cards into defense. Since the event is searchable through the Straw Hat engine, it lets you convert a card that's weak in the current spot into +3000 counter and stabilize clunky hands much more easily.",
		cards: [badManners],
	},
	{
		copy:
			"Managing DON!! carefully matters a lot in this deck. Several lines ask you to preserve or attach DON!! at exact moments, whether that's to turn on Sabo's effect, make your attackers hit the right thresholds, or keep your defensive events available. It's also why some tech cards are hard to fit: there just isn't much DON!! to waste.",
		cards: [donCard],
	},
];
---

<MainLayout title={title} description={description}>
	<section class="hero guide-hero">
		<div class="container hero-grid">
			<div>
				<span class="hero-badge">Deck Guide</span>
				<h1>Sabo OP13 Deck Guide</h1>
				<p>
					Sabo OP13 gets a real lift from ST31, which opens new ways to reinforce the deck's early engine and
					makes its wide-board turns much more consistent. The core plan is still to snowball through Sanji into
					Pirates Docking Six, but the starter cards give the deck better search density, smoother pressure, and
					more reliable follow-up once the first combo lands.
				</p>
				<div class="hero-actions">
					<a class="btn btn-primary" href="#curve">Curve Guide</a>
					<a class="btn btn-secondary" href="#matchups">Matchups</a>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="guide-icon">
				<img src={icon} alt="Sabo OP13 guide icon" loading="lazy" />
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="decklist" class="list-section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-header decklist-header">
				<div class="decklist-title-row">
					<h2>Decklist</h2>
					<button class="btn btn-primary copy-sim-button" type="button" data-copy-sim data-sim-deck={simDeckCode}>
						Copy for Sim
					</button>
				</div>
				<p>Main list for Sabo OP13 in the OP16 format.</p>
			</div>
			<div class="deck-grid">
				{decklist.map((entry) => (
					<button
						class="deck-card"
						type="button"
						data-card-preview
						data-full={entry.img}
						data-name={`${entry.code} ${entry.name}`}
					>
						<img loading="lazy" src={entry.img} alt={`${entry.code} ${entry.name}`} />
						<div class="deck-info">
							<span class="deck-count">{entry.count}x</span>
							<div>
								<div class="deck-name">{entry.name}</div>
								<div class="deck-code">{entry.code}</div>
							</div>
						</div>
						<span class="deck-role">{entry.role}</span>
					</button>
				))}
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="mulligan" class="list-section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-header">
				<div>
					<h2>Mulligan</h2>
					<p>
						Your mulligan priority is finding your searchers. Their job is to assemble the two key pieces of your
						opening plan: ST31-001 Sanji and OP15-088 Pirates Docking Six.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="mulligan-stack">
				<div class="tip-card glow-block">
					<h3>Cards to Prioritize</h3>
					<div class="mulligan-rows">
						{mulliganKeeps.map((entry) => (
							<div class="mulligan-row">
								<div class="tip-cards">
									{entry.cards.map((entryCard) => (
										<button
											class="mini-card"
											type="button"
											data-card-preview
											data-full={entryCard.img}
											data-name={`${entryCard.code} ${entryCard.name}`}
										>
											<img loading="lazy" src={entryCard.img} alt={`${entryCard.code} ${entryCard.name}`} />
											<div class="mini-label">{entryCard.name}</div>
										</button>
									))}
								</div>
								<div class="mulligan-copy">
									<p>{entry.copy}</p>
								</div>
							</div>
						))}
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="tip-card glow-block">
					<h3>What to Look For</h3>
					<div class="tip-cards">
						<button
							class="mini-card"
							type="button"
							data-card-preview
							data-full={sanjiSt31.img}
							data-name={`${sanjiSt31.code} ${sanjiSt31.name}`}
						>
							<img loading="lazy" src={sanjiSt31.img} alt={`${sanjiSt31.code} ${sanjiSt31.name}`} />
							<div class="mini-label">{sanjiSt31.name}</div>
						</button>
						<button
							class="mini-card"
							type="button"
							data-card-preview
							data-full={piratesDockingSix.img}
							data-name={`${piratesDockingSix.code} ${piratesDockingSix.name}`}
						>
							<img loading="lazy" src={piratesDockingSix.img} alt={`${piratesDockingSix.code} ${piratesDockingSix.name}`} />
							<div class="mini-label">{piratesDockingSix.name}</div>
						</button>
					</div>
					<ul class="bullet-list">
						<li>The main goal is to assemble ST31-001 Sanji plus OP15-088 Pirates Docking Six.</li>
						<li>Pirates Docking Six matters even more because its self +6-cost effect is what gives you the cost-8 condition needed to turn on Sabo's Leader effect.</li>
						<li>Your searchers should prepare that sequence instead of chasing a random late-game card.</li>
						<li>An ideal opener has Nami, Bonney, or Thousand Sunny, plus Sanji and/or Pirates Docking Six already in hand.</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section id="curve" class="list-section">
		<div class="container">
			<div class="section-header">
				<div>
					<h2>Curve Guide</h2>
					<p>
						The main goal is assembling ST31-001 Sanji, OP15-088 Pirates Docking Six, and a Straw Hat Crew
						character that costs 2 or less from your trash for the Pirates Docking Six effect. Once that first
						sequence happens, the going-first and going-second curves start to look much closer, and the deck
						begins to flood the board with characters. Sabo's effect also makes the board much harder to break by
						giving your whole field +1000 power as long as you control a cost-8-or-higher character.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<div class="curve-block glow-block">
				<h3>Going First</h3>
				<div class="curve-rows">
					{curveFirst.map((slot) => (
						<div class="curve-row">
							<div class="curve-label">{slot.label}</div>
							<div class="curve-cards" style={`--curve-card-columns: ${slot.cards.length === 5 ? 3 : Math.min(slot.cards.length, 4)};`}>
